rcq("R08-B-08-4", "Strengthen / weaken / assumption", "Difficult",
    "Which of the following, if true, would most weaken the author's claim in paragraph 5 about what literacy rates chiefly depend on?",
    "With schooling, reading matter and rewards for reading held equal, societies using regularly spelled alphabets had far higher adult literacy.",
    [("Children learning an alphabet with regular spelling begin decoding unfamiliar words earlier than children learning a script that represents meaning.",
      "Paragraph 3 already concedes this advantage of economy; an earlier start in decoding does not show that literacy rates depend chiefly on script."),
     ("Several societies using alphabets but lacking widespread schooling have had low adult literacy for long periods.",
      "This fits the author's claim that schooling matters more than script, so it supports rather than weakens paragraph 5."),
     ("Learners of English must memorise many irregularly spelled words almost as wholes before they can read fluently.",
      "Paragraph 3 makes this point itself; it concerns the cost of one alphabet and does not bear on what literacy rates chiefly depend on.")],
    "Paragraph 5 claims that literacy rates 'owe much more to schools, to the availability of reading matter and to whether reading pays' than to scripts. If, with those conditions held equal, script type still produced large differences in literacy, script would emerge as a major cause, which directly undercuts the claim.",
    "Choosing the decoding option because it shows an advantage of alphabets, without noticing that the author grants it and that it concerns speed of learning, not rates of literacy.",
    "Requires holding the claim's comparative form ('much more') and seeing that only a controlled comparison tests it; the most tempting wrong option is a conceded point.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

